<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abomey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abomey is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey</a> from the seventeenth century until 1894. Each king built his own palace there, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey">Royal Palaces of Abomey</a>, with their painted clay bas-reliefs recording the kingdom's history, were made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1985. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behanzin">Behanzin</a> burned much of the town before the French entered it in November 1892.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abomey.jpg" alt="Abomey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ji-Elle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rue_d%27Abomey_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Abomey, also called Agbome, was founded in the 17th century as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey</a>, on the site of the former village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kana">Kana</a>. Traditional legends say the town was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/do-aklin">Do-Aklin</a>, a son of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a> who ventured north to found his own kingdom. The name is thought to come from Danhomé, meaning belly of Dan, Dan being the original chief of the village.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> expanded rapidly in the 1700s, absorbing many surrounding kingdoms and growing rich from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. In the late 18th century a dry ditch alone surrounded Abomey, while the royal palaces had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> wall 20 feet high. By the 1840s it had an estimated population of 24,000. The French conquered the kingdom on 16 November 1892, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behanzin">Béhanzin</a> torched the city and fled northward. The town then declined, as the French built a new administrative centre at Bohicon, to the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Abomey is the capital of the Zou Department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and remains a centre for crafts. It houses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-palaces-of-abomey">Royal Palaces of Abomey</a>, earthen structures built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fon-people">Fon people</a> between the mid-17th and late 19th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> states that twelve kings ruled from 1625 to 1900, each building a palace within the same cob-wall area, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akaba">Akaba</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behanzin">Béhanzin</a> the palaces covered some 44 hectares behind a wall 10 metres high and 4 kilometres long; only two of the twelve palaces survived the burning of 1892. The site became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1985. Fifty of the 56 bas-reliefs of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glele">Glèlè</a>'s palace have been replaced on the rebuilt structure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abomey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to its royal traditions, a succession dispute in the Kingdom of Allada early in the seventeenth century led one branch of the royal family to move north to the <b>Abomey</b> plateau, where it settled among the Fon and other local peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dahomey, the kingdom of the Fon on the <b>Abomey</b> plateau, ranked its people around the king and his palaces at Abomey.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dahomey">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Dahomey</a></li><li><blockquote>After wars with the Kingdom of Dahomey in 1890 and 1892, General Alfred-Amedee Dodds took <b>Abomey</b>, and King Behanzin surrendered in 1894.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-dahomey">French Dahomey</a></li></ul></section>
